                  "statement": "The new Prime Minister's security moves this week leaned towards European partners (Germany on hybrid threats and the Global Combat Air Programme, the EU ahead of the 20 November summit) while the US pursued its own track with Russia.",
                  "supersedes": ""
                },
                {
                  "facts": [
                    "S8:F31",
                    "S8:F34",
                    "S8:F39",
                    "S9:F35",
                    "S11:F37",
                    "S13:F51",
                    "S14:F56",
                    "F57",
                    "F52"
                  ],
                  "ref": "N3",
                  "statement": "The week's domestic announcements share one method that matches the Prime Minister's Greater Manchester record: concentrate public investment in one place, as with Derby's railway headquarters, apprenticeships and the Alstom fleet, and deliver it through regional partnerships, as with No10 North in Scotland.",
                  "supersedes": ""
                },
                {
                  "facts": [
                    "S13:F51",
                    "S14:F56"
                  ],
                  "ref": "N4",
                  "statement": "The Scottish Government matching the UK's £10 million for two defence technical colleges is the first concrete case of the joint working the Prime Minister asked Holyrood for, and it came in the same week as his offer.",
                  "supersedes": ""
                }
              ],
              "statements": [
                {
                  "cites": [
                    "F20",
                    "F55"
                  ],
                  "text": "This was the week Andy Burnham, who took over in Downing Street in July, set out a first autumn agenda abroad and at home."
                },
                {
                  "cites": [
                    "S1:F2",
                    "S1:F7",
                    "S2:F6"
                  ],
                  "text": "The US and Russia reached an agreement on 9 October, and two days later President Trump announced a reciprocal energy ceasefire. The next day the UK widened its sanctions to cover over 90% of Russia's oil producing capacity."
                },
                {
                  "cites": [
                    "S6:F22",
                    "S7:F29",
                    "F30",
                    "S6:F21"
                  ],
                  "text": "Zelenskyy's visit to Downing Street followed. The UK is delivering £45 million of surface-to-air missiles, set out a new package of Lightweight Multirole Missiles and discussed speeding up defensive support before winter. Ministers have been told to draw up more sanctions, military aid and fiscal packages."
                },
                {
                  "cites": [
                    "N1",
                    "F1"
                  ],
                  "text": "Taken together, London answered the US–Russia de-escalation by adding pressure, not easing it. The Record shows a UK spokesperson responded to the agreement but does not hold what was said, so how far this was a deliberate counterweight is still open."
                },
                {
                  "cites": [
                    "S4:F9",
                    "F11",
                    "S4:F10",
                    "F16",
                    "S5:F17",
                    "N2"
                  ],
                  "text": "The security picture leans towards Europe: a hybrid-threats partnership with Chancellor Merz, openness to Germany joining the Global Combat Air Programme, a National Centre for Information Defence, and a meeting with von der Leyen ahead of the UK-EU summit in Brussels on 20 November."
                },
                {
                  "cites": [
                    "S8:F31",
                    "S8:F34",
                    "S9:F35",
                    "S11:F37",
                    "S8:F39"
                  ],
                  "text": "At home, Derby became a railway cluster within one announcement: Great British Railways' headquarters for up to 450 staff, up to 600 apprentices, and a £1 billion northern fleet at Alstom's Derby site, with transport links delivered alongside the city council and the combined authority."
                },
                {
                  "cites": [
                    "S13:F51",
                    "F57",
                    "F52",
                    "N3"
                  ],
                  "text": "In Scotland the Prime Minister offered to work with Holyrood through No10 North and drew on his Greater Manchester record, including buying buses built in Falkirk. The common method is to concentrate investment in places and run it through regional partnerships."
                },
                {
                  "cites": [
                    "S14:F56",
                    "N4"
                  ],
                  "text": "The early test has passed on a small scale: Holyrood agreed to match the UK's £10 million for two defence technical colleges. That ties the defence push to the Scotland offer, and is the thread to watch before the EU summit."
                }
